sections.s2_technical:
Interfaces are inherited. If a parent class declares `implements Serializable`, that capability flows to all subclasses, and there is no Java syntax to say a subclass is not serializable while its parent is. So you must enforce the restriction with behavior rather than with the type system.

The mechanism uses the special serialization hook methods. During serialization the engine looks for a private method `writeObject(ObjectOutputStream)` on the class, and during deserialization a private method `readObject(ObjectInputStream)`. If you define these in the subclass and throw NotSerializableException from them, any attempt to serialize or deserialize an instance of that subclass fails:

```java
class VaultAccount extends Account { // Account is Serializable → VaultAccount inherits it
    private void writeObject(java.io.ObjectOutputStream out) throws java.io.IOException {
        throw new java.io.NotSerializableException(VaultAccount.class.getName());
    }
    private void readObject(java.io.ObjectInputStream in) throws java.io.IOException {
        throw new java.io.NotSerializableException(VaultAccount.class.getName());
    }
}
```

Now `oos.writeObject(new VaultAccount(...))` throws NotSerializableException naming VaultAccount, and so does any attempt to read such bytes back. The parent Account and any other normal subclasses remain fully serializable.

A subtle point is why you override both methods. Overriding only writeObject blocks creating the bytes, but if bytes were somehow produced, readObject would still try to read them, so for a complete and symmetric guarantee you block both directions. Throwing from both means neither serialization nor deserialization of that subclass can ever succeed.

A senior point is that this is a deliberate security pattern in banking: certain highly sensitive entities, like a vault or an internal settlement account, must never be flattened to bytes that could be cached, logged, or transmitted, so the subclass hard-fails serialization even though its convenient parent is serializable. Another senior point is that this is cleaner than trying to mark every field transient, because a future developer could add a new non-transient field and reopen the hole, whereas throwing from writeObject and readObject blocks the entire operation regardless of fields.

